==Latin==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|la|jam}}

===Etymology===
One old acc.sg.f. (as {{m|la|tam}}, {{m|la|quam}}, {{m|la|nam}}), from one {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} stem {{m|ine-pro|*yo-}}, thematic derivation of the anaphoric stem {{m|ine-pro|*éy|i-, ey-}}. See also Ancient Greek {{m|grc|ὅς}}, Sanskrit {{m|sa|यद्|tr=yás, yā, yad|sc=Deva}}, Avestan {{m|ae|𐬫𐬋|tr=yō}}, Phrygian {{m|xpg|ιος|sc=polytonic|tr=yos}}, {{cog|got|𐌾𐌰}}, {{m|got|𐌾𐌰𐌹||yes}}, {{cog|goh|ja|ja, jā|yes}} ({{cog|de|ja}}), {{cog|ang|ġēa||yea, yes}} ({{cog|en|yea}}). More at {{l|en|yes}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Classical}} {{IPA|/ˈjam/|lang=la}}
* {{audio|la-cls-iam.ogg|Audio (Classical)|lang=la}}

===Adverb===
{{la-adv||iam|-}}

# [[already]]
#: {{ux|la|Birota '''iam''' refecta est.|t=The bicycle has '''already''' been repaired.}}
# [[now]]
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Curculio|5.3.5|quote=CAPPADOX: '''Iam iam''' faciam ut iusseris.<ref>[http://www.thelatinlibrary.com/plautus/curculio.shtml Latin]</ref>|trans=CAPPADOX: '''Here''', '''here''', I’ll do as you say!<ref>[http://artemis.austincollege.edu/acad/cml/rcape/comedy/5-3.html English]</ref>}}
# [[anymore]]

====Usage notes====
{{m|la|iam|Iam}} means, generally, “at some point previous” or “since some point previous”. In English, {{m|en|already}}, the most common translation, is used only to emphasize that this point might have been expected to be later, whereas {{m|en|now}} is used to emphasize that the statement was once false, even when the statement refers to a point in the past or future. {{m|la|iam|Iam}} is used to express either. (Likewise, the most common Latin word for {{m|en|now}}, {{m|la|nunc}}, denotes only the literal present moment.) Also, where {{m|la|iam}} means {{m|en|now}}, it is often used in negative sentences, in which the most common English construction uses {{m|en|anymore}}.

